     "<br><br>On Sunday, April 21, 2013 7:17:57 PM UTC-4, Phil Chalmers wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"Arial\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:10pt\"><i>Email question:</i> </span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial;line-height:18px\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial;line-height:18px\">Using your package ‘</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial;line-height:18px;background-color:rgb(255,255,204)\">mirt</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial;line-height:18px\">’, is it possible to do a bifactor analysis where some of the items only load on the general factor and on none of the specific factors?</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial;line-height:18px\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:Arial;line-height:18px\"><i>Reply</i>: </span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.800000190734863px\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.800000190734863px\">Yes that it possible, though you&#39;ll be required to declare an arbitrary specific vector anyway and modify the internal elements manually. For example, use</span></p><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">######</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">sv &lt;- bfactor(data, specific, pars = &#39;values&#39;)</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">sv #modify the elements you want in here, changing their values and changing est to FALSE</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">sv$value[2] &lt;-  0<br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">sv$est[2] &lt;-  FALSE</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">mod &lt;- bfactor(data, specific, pars = sv)</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">#####</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.800000190734863px\">Hope that helps.</div><div><font size=\"2\" face=\"Arial\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size:10pt\"><br></span></font></div></blockquote>"
